import ContentPage from "../layouts/ContentPage.astro";
import PullQuote from "../components/PullQuote.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
---
<ContentPage
  title="Publish"
  active="publish"
  kicker="Boundary III · Publish"
  headline="You are reading the third boundary."
  stand="Nothing about the lab crossed into this page. That is the whole mechanism."
>
  <p>
    This page is an artifact of the boundary it describes, and the honest description is
    simpler than the one you might expect. There is no filter standing between the lab's
    data and this site, because no lab data is sent to it. Every word here was written by
    hand. The site is a human account of the architecture, not a rendering of the
    inventory that architecture is built around.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is a claim about the pipeline, and it should not be read as a stronger one. Nothing
    here can leak <em>by ingestion</em>: no automated path carries a hostname, an address or a
    credential from the lab into this build. It does not follow that nothing can leak at all.
    An author who types a real identifier into a page ships it, because the build compiles what
    it is given and nothing scans the result. Hand-authoring moves the risk from a mechanism to
    a person; the tripwire described below is what would move it back.
  </p>

  <h2>Nothing crossed, so nothing had to be caught</h2>

  <p>
    The lab's source of truth lives in <code>inventory.yaml</code> inside the
    <a href="/single-source-of-truth">Describe boundary</a>. That file holds every host,
    network, VLAN, service route, and known issue in the lab. It is not published here.
    It is not copied here with sensitive fields removed. It is not read by this site's
    build at all: no code path in this repository opens it, imports it, or receives
    anything derived from it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href="/explorer">explorer's</a> physical layer is the one place a reader might
    reasonably expect real lab data, and it is not. It is a curated, deliberately generic
    stand-in — invented aliases, no real hostnames, addresses, or identifiers — hand-placed
    to show the shape of the architecture rather than the contents of the rack. The source
    file says as much in its opening comment, so a future contributor cannot mistake it for
    generated output.
  </p>

  <PullQuote>Secrets are not scrubbed from this site. They were never compiled into it.</PullQuote>

  <h2>What the boundary is designed to become</h2>

  <p>
    Hand-authoring is a strong guarantee and a weak pipeline. It cannot drift into leaking
    something, but it also cannot stay current on its own: every fact on this site is a fact
    a human re-checked. The intended end state closes that gap with two mechanisms, and it
    is worth being precise that both are <strong>designed and not yet built</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The first is an <strong>allowlist projection</strong>. Each field in the inventory would
    carry a publication status; fields marked publishable cross the boundary and all others
    do not exist in the build context. The point of a projection rather than a redaction is
    that a redaction is a scrub that can be done wrong, while a projection only ever emits
    what is explicitly named. Adding a field to the inventory would keep it private by
    default until an author deliberately marked it otherwise: fail-closed, not fail-open.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second is an independent <strong>tripwire</strong> over the build output — a scan
    for anything secret-shaped, run after the projection and before the site is assembled,
    failing the build on a match. Its value is that it is not the projection. Two mechanisms
    would have to fail together for a secret to reach the public, and neither one's authors
    can talk the other out of its verdict.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="publish" label="Status of this boundary">
    <p>
      Neither the projection nor the tripwire exists today. No field in
      <code>inventory.yaml</code> carries a publication marker, no generator emits a public
      projection, and this repository's build runs no output scan. The safety property this
      page claims rests entirely on the fact that <em>no lab data is imported here at all</em>
      — which is airtight while it holds, and stops holding on the day someone wires the
      inventory in. That is the day the two mechanisms above have to exist, and the reason
      they are described here as a commitment rather than an accomplishment.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Static by construction</h2>

  <p>
    What ships is a fully static build: compiled HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. No server, no
    database, no server-side rendering touching live data. There is no runtime connection to
    the lab, no webhook listening for state changes, no endpoint that reflects anything
    internal. The pipeline terminates at publication. It does not reach back.
  </p>

  <p>
    That property does not depend on the two mechanisms above and will survive them. Whatever
    eventually crosses this boundary crosses it once, at build time, in one direction, and
    arrives as inert text.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="publish" label="You are here">
    <p>
      This site is Boundary III. It exists because the other two boundaries work: the
      <a href="/single-source-of-truth">single source of truth</a> in Describe holds the
      facts, and <a href="/operate">Operate</a> acts on them under a ceiling it cannot
      exceed. The lab has considerably more state than what is described here. What is not
      described is not hidden behind this page. It was never sent to it. See the
      <a href="/colophon">colophon</a> for how the build pipeline is assembled.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</ContentPage>
